WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.280
Choosing a local vision model is an engineering decision, not a popularity contest.

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:09.670
Vision-language models differ across OCR, objects, counting, charts, screenshots,

00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:11.840
descriptions, and visual question answering.

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:13.885
The use case defines which differences matter.

00:00:13.885 --> 00:00:18.060
Hardware fit includes VRAM or unified memory, system RAM, accelerator support,

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:19.880
concurrency, and operational headroom.

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:23.790
Model size, quantization, image-token budget, runtime, prompt template,

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:26.136
and thinking mode form one tested configuration.

00:00:26.136 --> 00:00:30.580
Accuracy must be considered with completion, latency, memory failures, hallucinations,

00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:33.870
OCR and counting errors, and consistency across repeated runs.

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:37.745
A smaller or lower-scoring model can be better when it is stable and fast enough for the

00:00:37.745 --> 00:00:38.300
workflow.

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:42.245
Benchmark locally using representative images, prewritten rubrics, repeated runs,

00:00:42.245 --> 00:00:44.930
controlled settings, and a durable evaluation log.

00:00:44.930 --> 00:00:49.139
Community benchmarks provide hypotheses and hardware tiers, not permanent answers.

00:00:49.139 --> 00:00:54.410
Deployment adds privacy, licensing, retention, cost, power, monitoring, fallback, updates,

00:00:54.410 --> 00:00:55.455
and human review.

00:00:55.455 --> 00:00:58.100
Keep important decisions grounded in visible evidence.

00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:02.230
The best model is the configuration that meets task quality and reliability on available

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:05.680
hardware while preserving privacy and accountable human judgment.
